
  - [1mExplicit [38;5;153m--format csv|json[0m — unambiguous and trivially testable; [38;5;153m--out[39m can be anything, including a path with no
    extension or [38;5;153m-[39m. Costs one more thing to type.
  - [1mInfer from [38;5;153m--out[39m extension[0m — [38;5;153mreport.csv[39m just works, nothing redundant to type. But [38;5;153m--out report.txt[39m has to either
    error or guess, and a typo'd extension silently produces the wrong format in an unattended cron job.
  - [1mInfer, with [38;5;153m--format[39m as an override[0m — ergonomic and escapable, but now two things can specify the format and you
    need a rule for when they disagree.

  [1mMy recommendation: explicit [38;5;153m--format[39m, required.[0m This runs unattended from cron, where a silent wrong-format write is
  discovered by whatever consumes the file, hours later. Being required also means no default to change later if a third
  format ever appears. Inference stays addable as a default-when-omitted.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should `export` determine the output format? → Required --format flag (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mDesign: [38;5;153mreportkit export[0m

  [1mCommand[0m

  reportkit export --day YYYY-MM-DD --format csv|json --out PATH

  All three required. [38;5;153msummarize[39m is untouched — plain text stays a stdout-only thing.

  [1m[38;5;153msrc/reportkit/summarize.py[0m — two new renderers alongside [38;5;153mrender_text[39m, same shape as it: pure functions, rows in,
  string out.

  - [38;5;153mrender_csv(rows) -> str[39m — stdlib [38;5;153mcsv[39m into a [38;5;153mStringIO[39m, header [38;5;153maccount_id,cents[39m, one row per account, no total row.
    [38;5;153mlineterminator="\n"[39m so we don't get [38;5;153m\r\n[39m in a file written on a Linux billing host.
  - [38;5;153mrender_json(rows, day) -> str[39m — [38;5;153m{"day": ..., "total_cents": ..., "accounts": [{"account_id": ..., "cents": ...}]}[39m,
    [38;5;153mindent=2[39m, trailing newline. The total is summed the same way [38;5;153mrender_text[39m does, so the file and the email can't
    disagree.

  Note the asymmetry: JSON needs [38;5;153mday[39m, CSV doesn't. I'd rather have honest signatures than pad CSV with an unused
  argument.


